WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:10.019
Apple two. Banana four. Orange one. Grapes
one. Which fruit did most children choose?

2
00:00:10.239 --> 00:00:14.744
Draw those four numbers as columns,
and you can see the answer at a glance.

3
00:00:15.244 --> 00:00:20.677
Your class answered a survey about party
fruit. The answers went into a table.

4
00:00:20.897 --> 00:00:25.402
A table holds the numbers in
rows. Apple two. Banana four.

5
00:00:25.622 --> 00:00:29.662
Orange one. Grapes one. Every
number in the table is exact.

6
00:00:29.882 --> 00:00:33.272
To find the winner you have
to read every single number.

7
00:00:33.772 --> 00:00:36.884
A block graph shows the
same data as columns.

8
00:00:37.104 --> 00:00:40.912
One column for each fruit.
One block for each answer.

9
00:00:41.132 --> 00:00:44.708
Banana is the tallest column,
so banana is the answer.

10
00:00:44.928 --> 00:00:47.575
You saw it before you
read a single number.

11
00:00:47.795 --> 00:00:52.300
The graph holds no more data than the
table. It just makes it easy to see.

12
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.654
The computer builds the graph
from the table, column by column.

13
00:00:56.874 --> 00:01:00.496
Apple two: two blocks.
Banana four: four blocks.

14
00:01:00.716 --> 00:01:05.268
Orange one block. Grapes one
block. Every column is built.

15
00:01:05.488 --> 00:01:09.296
A column stops when it has as
many blocks as the table says.

16
00:01:09.516 --> 00:01:12.999
Now the pattern shows itself,
and the tallest column wins.

17
00:01:13.499 --> 00:01:16.935
The playground is too
crowded. That is a problem.

18
00:01:17.155 --> 00:01:20.870
So the class counted the children
playing football each day.

19
00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:27.453
Monday nine. Tuesday four. Wednesday
seven. Thursday three. Friday eight.

20
00:01:27.673 --> 00:01:31.620
Monday has nine. That is the
biggest number in the table.

21
00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:35.277
Thursday has three. That
is the smallest number.

22
00:01:35.497 --> 00:01:39.908
So football club moves to Thursday.
Read the data, then decide.

23
00:01:40.408 --> 00:01:43.473
Grown-ups solve problems
with data every day.

24
00:01:43.693 --> 00:01:48.012
A shop counts what sells. Bananas
sell out, so it orders more.

25
00:01:48.232 --> 00:01:53.526
A doctor charts a temperature. A city
counts cars before building a crossing.

26
00:01:53.746 --> 00:01:57.647
A school kitchen counts lunches,
so it cooks the right amount.

27
00:01:58.147 --> 00:02:01.677
Why keep the class data on
a computer, not on paper?

28
00:02:01.897 --> 00:02:06.169
The paper list got wet. The copy
on the computer is still there.

29
00:02:06.389 --> 00:02:11.173
Find one name out of five hundred?
The computer searches it in a blink.

30
00:02:11.393 --> 00:02:15.201
It copies the table, sends it,
and draws the graph in a blink.

31
00:02:15.701 --> 00:02:19.741
A table holds the numbers. A
graph shows them as columns.

32
00:02:19.961 --> 00:02:23.676
The tallest column is the most,
and you see it at a glance.

33
00:02:23.896 --> 00:02:28.215
Read the data first, then decide.
That is solving a problem.

34
00:02:28.435 --> 00:02:31.639
And a computer keeps it
safe, and draws the graph.
